{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1788369081,"nonce":"WRGyESAmArMoQ0HwTYMY5Zftg8w=","target":"15842b982717981ab58904cbddb9f0a69f514d3d1d0389cde43b563fc70669eb","message":"Evan, watching the bench on 2026-09-02 after `b7359fa` landed: *\"that's deterministic, it should tell you what's on the ground and next to that if it's forbidden or in a forbidden area — should this framework be anywhere else?\"*\n\n\n**As first filed, this issue claimed `Materials.cs` was blind to the allowed area and that `place-layout --dry-run`'s `available` figure counted material nobody could reach. That is false**, and it was the issue's most alarming example. Caught on the bench the same evening by reading a real shortfall envelope rather than trusting a grep:\n\n```\n\"availability_basis\": \"reachable-unforbidden-by-a-colonist\",\n\"forbidden\": 300,\n\"hint\": \"there is enough of this on the map, but 300 of it is FORBIDDEN — `unforbid` …\"\n```\n\n`Materials.cs` uses `IsForbidden(Thing, Pawn)` and `CanReach` per builder — its own comment at line 102 says *\"Reaching is a per-pawn question\"* — and `IsForbidden(Thing, Pawn)` folds in `InAllowedArea` via `t.PositionHeld.IsForbidden(pawn)`. Line 203 even switches its published basis honestly: `\"reachable-unforbidden-by-a-colonist\"` when builders exist, `\"faction-unforbidden\"` when they do not. The `IsForbidden(Faction.OfPlayer)` at line 225 computes a separate *context* count, not the availability figure.\n\nThe original filing generalised from a grep for `IsForbidden(Faction` without reading what each hit was for. Recorded rather than silently edited, because this round has now caught three false headlines — `eef837a`, `855117a`, and this one — and the pattern is worth the embarrassment.\n\n\nThe game has three answers:\n\n- `IsForbidden(t, Faction)` — the `CompForbiddable` flag only.\n- `IsForbidden(t, Pawn)` — the flag **plus the allowed area**, plus lord and ritual reservations.\n- separately, `CanReach` and `CanReserve`.\n\n**Doing it right:** `DesignateReach.cs` (per pawn, area-aware, added by `b7359fa`) and `Materials.cs` (per builder, reach-aware, with an honest basis string).\n\n**Still answering the weaker question:**\n\n| file | what it answers | cost of the weaker answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BillIngredients.cs` | can this bill be worked | `:491` and `:608` use `IsForbidden(Faction.OfPlayer)`; its own header at `:46` records the choice. A corpse outside every colonist's area reads as usable — **mechanism F2, the one that killed Marco**, in a second verb |\n| `FoodRot.cs` | how much food the colony has | `food_days` can count food nobody can walk to |\n| `ThingVerbs.cs` | `things` rollups | the same wherever a caller counts stock |\n\n`BillIngredients.cs` is the sharp one, and not only because of F2: it publishes `clauses_not_checked: [\"reachable\", \"reservable\"]`, which is an honest declaration of its limits — **with the area silently absent from it**. A declared-complete answer with an undeclared hole is worse than an obviously partial one.\n\n\n1. `BillIngredients.cs` either asks the pawn-scoped question or adds `area` to `clauses_not_checked`. **Either is acceptable**; silently answering the weaker question is not.\n2. If it asks the pawn-scoped question, it reuses the route `DesignateReach.cs` already holds — the guarded `EffectiveAreaRestrictionInPawnCurrentMap` accessor and the `TrueCount == 0` short-circuit. **One implementation, not a second copy.** Two copies of one idea is `927be4f`, filed this round for exactly that.\n3. `FoodRot.cs` and `ThingVerbs.cs` publish their scope the way `Materials.cs` publishes `availability_basis` — a caller must be able to tell which question was answered. Stating the limit is an acceptable answer for a cheap read path.\n4. Bench command list (markdown, not a Python suite): bind every colonist to a narrow area, leave a corpse outside it, assert `bills` reports it unusable or declares the area unchecked.\n\n\nA measurement-honesty issue. No verb should start refusing because of it. `33bc796` covers the `prioritize` half and should be read alongside.","files":null}]}